1
00:00:01,920 --> 00:00:02,820
Hello again.

2
00:00:03,060 --> 00:00:05,610
This is the lesson about passive voice.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:11,700
So this is another big and nice grammar topic that we're going to speak about.

4
00:00:12,900 --> 00:00:21,900
Passive voice is not a very easy grammar topic, but we need to use passive voice in a number of situations,

5
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:30,270
especially when we are talking about things like reports, things like status reports, for example,

6
00:00:30,270 --> 00:00:39,360
bug reports in testing, if you work in testing and basically anywhere where you have to give some kind

7
00:00:39,360 --> 00:00:48,030
of a more formalized statuses or text information about something, passive voice is going to be very,

8
00:00:48,030 --> 00:00:49,080
very useful.

9
00:00:49,710 --> 00:00:54,090
And I'm sure you already use passive voice in a lot of situations.

10
00:00:54,090 --> 00:01:02,670
So let's just have an overview of which tenses and tense forms and verb forms you can use to create

11
00:01:02,670 --> 00:01:03,930
passive sentences.

12
00:01:05,170 --> 00:01:10,780
So first of all, let's look at these two pairs of sentences.

13
00:01:11,320 --> 00:01:19,090
The first pair is I log hours every week and Hours are logged every week.

14
00:01:19,960 --> 00:01:24,040
So both of these sentences are in the same tense.

15
00:01:24,640 --> 00:01:29,460
The difference between them is that one sentence is active voice.

16
00:01:29,470 --> 00:01:32,260
In the second sentence is the passive voice.

17
00:01:32,830 --> 00:01:34,990
The second pair is

18
00:01:34,990 --> 00:01:42,370
My colleague created this presentation and This presentation was created by my colleague.

19
00:01:42,760 --> 00:01:44,650
Again, the same situation.

20
00:01:44,650 --> 00:01:46,080
Two sentences.

21
00:01:46,090 --> 00:01:49,010
They are describing the same thing.

22
00:01:49,030 --> 00:01:57,070
The idea of both sentences is the same, but they are said in two different ways.

23
00:01:57,070 --> 00:02:00,430
And the difference is again, first is active,

24
00:02:00,460 --> 00:02:01,720
the second is passive.

25
00:02:02,770 --> 00:02:10,449
The main question that I want you to focus here is what tends is the first pair and what tense is the

26
00:02:10,449 --> 00:02:11,230
second pair.

27
00:02:12,010 --> 00:02:16,810
So I'm sure you've already answered this question.

28
00:02:16,810 --> 00:02:23,800
So the this slide shows us, yes, the first pair is present simple and the second pair is past simple.

29
00:02:24,040 --> 00:02:29,450
Now, how do we know that an active sentence is in present simple?

30
00:02:29,470 --> 00:02:32,530
Well, of course, we look at the main verb.

31
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:34,480
We look at the verb log.

32
00:02:34,510 --> 00:02:35,620
I log.

33
00:02:35,620 --> 00:02:37,030
I do it regularly.

34
00:02:37,030 --> 00:02:38,660
So, yes, it's present

35
00:02:38,660 --> 00:02:39,190
simple.

36
00:02:39,610 --> 00:02:46,110
And then the passive sentence we have Hours are logged every week.

37
00:02:46,120 --> 00:02:56,140
So here we have a bigger construction, a bigger formation of this tense and voice.

38
00:02:56,560 --> 00:02:59,050
So we have are and logged.

39
00:02:59,410 --> 00:03:05,250
Which of those two words actually says that the sentence is in present

40
00:03:05,260 --> 00:03:05,890
simple?

41
00:03:07,510 --> 00:03:13,960
This first word gives us this information, because are is the present simple of the word

42
00:03:13,990 --> 00:03:14,710
be.

43
00:03:14,710 --> 00:03:15,390
Of the verb 

44
00:03:15,410 --> 00:03:15,830
be.

45
00:03:17,050 --> 00:03:20,140
Both of them are in present simple.

46
00:03:20,140 --> 00:03:21,690
And the second pair.

47
00:03:21,700 --> 00:03:23,840
Both of them are in past simple.

48
00:03:23,860 --> 00:03:30,700
We see that my colleague created.. This is the past. And the presentation

49
00:03:30,700 --> 00:03:34,090
again, the past form of be - was.

50
00:03:34,960 --> 00:03:39,250
That's why we know it's past simple - was created by my colleague.

51
00:03:41,530 --> 00:03:49,570
Now, what is the main difference generally, logically, between the active and the passive voices?

52
00:03:50,410 --> 00:03:57,630
If we look at those two sentences my colleague created, what do we have here?

53
00:03:57,640 --> 00:04:00,670
We have the doer of the action.

54
00:04:00,670 --> 00:04:09,550
The doer is somebody or something that does the action and the action is "created".

55
00:04:09,850 --> 00:04:13,630
So my colleague did something - he created.

56
00:04:13,790 --> 00:04:22,270
So the action kind of you can imagine that the arrow, the direction of the action is this way.

57
00:04:22,270 --> 00:04:25,180
So my colleague did something, created.

58
00:04:25,630 --> 00:04:29,560
And the second sentence, it's the same idea.

59
00:04:29,560 --> 00:04:36,310
But we change the word order and we put presentation to the beginning of the sentence.

60
00:04:36,310 --> 00:04:43,450
It means that my focus in this sentence is concentrated on presentation, but not on the colleague.

61
00:04:43,570 --> 00:04:46,960
And in this case, the sentence is vice versa.

62
00:04:47,320 --> 00:04:51,010
We start from the object, but not the doer of the action.

63
00:04:51,340 --> 00:04:59,170
And here we have to understand one simple and logical thing that this action - was created -

64
00:04:59,170 --> 00:05:04,030
this action is done on this object.

65
00:05:04,540 --> 00:05:14,200
So the action you can imagine the arrow going from here to here, from the action to the object, back

66
00:05:14,200 --> 00:05:15,130
to the object.

67
00:05:15,140 --> 00:05:18,820
So the presentation didn't do anything.

68
00:05:18,820 --> 00:05:24,790
It didn't perform any action, but the action was performed on the presentation.

69
00:05:24,790 --> 00:05:27,130
So it goes kind of back.

70
00:05:28,330 --> 00:05:35,800
And then if we want to still include the doer of the action in this into the sentence, we can include

71
00:05:35,800 --> 00:05:45,940
him at the end of the sentence and we introduce the doer of the action with the word by, by my colleague.

72
00:05:45,940 --> 00:05:47,140
By whom?

73
00:05:49,390 --> 00:05:49,810
Good.

74
00:05:50,680 --> 00:05:59,830
These two tenses, present simple and past simple, are very common to use with passive voice, when we put the

75
00:05:59,830 --> 00:06:02,380
object at the beginning of the sentence.

76
00:06:02,380 --> 00:06:02,690
Yeah.

77
00:06:02,740 --> 00:06:04,080
So I think these

78
00:06:04,240 --> 00:06:05,650
tenses are pretty okay.

79
00:06:05,660 --> 00:06:11,110
Most of you probably use those tenses on the daily basis.

80
00:06:11,120 --> 00:06:15,110
Yeah, because it's very common and very widespread.

81
00:06:16,340 --> 00:06:24,140
However, in this lesson we are going to look a little bit deeper and we will look at more complex tenses

82
00:06:24,140 --> 00:06:26,660
like continuous tenses and perfect tenses

83
00:06:27,350 --> 00:06:36,110
to see what is the formation of those sentences and how to use them, and how to have this logic of

84
00:06:36,110 --> 00:06:38,930
transforming active into passive and back.

85
00:06:40,130 --> 00:06:45,680
Before we do that, let's have a quick reminder of what is the formation of passive voice.

86
00:06:45,680 --> 00:06:48,110
And the formation is very simple.

87
00:06:48,110 --> 00:06:56,300
If you imagine it as kind of a formula, we have the word be, the verb, yes, and then we have past participle. So

88
00:06:56,450 --> 00:06:58,700
be plus past participle.

89
00:06:59,150 --> 00:07:00,650
What is past participle?

90
00:07:00,650 --> 00:07:03,580
It's the verb with -ed ending.

91
00:07:03,740 --> 00:07:06,770
So it's basically the past form of the verb.

92
00:07:07,040 --> 00:07:11,720
Or if the verb is irregular, we use the third column.

93
00:07:11,720 --> 00:07:15,800
Remember we have three columns, for example, sell - sold - sold.

94
00:07:16,640 --> 00:07:20,060
And so we use this third column for passive voice.

95
00:07:21,770 --> 00:07:22,280
Good.

96
00:07:22,280 --> 00:07:33,230
So if we want to try to imagine into which forms this formula can be transformed, we can go to next

97
00:07:33,230 --> 00:07:41,360
slide and we can see that be can actually be in the variety of different tenses and forms.

98
00:07:42,790 --> 00:07:50,830
So we could transform be into am, is or are, and in this case it's going to be present

99
00:07:50,830 --> 00:07:51,520
simple.

100
00:07:52,330 --> 00:07:57,580
If we transform be into was, were, what tense is it going to be?

101
00:07:58,600 --> 00:08:00,640
It's going to be past simple.

102
00:08:00,640 --> 00:08:01,330
Yes.

103
00:08:01,720 --> 00:08:11,140
If we transform be into have been or has been what tense is it - have, has been? It's present

104
00:08:11,140 --> 00:08:11,950
perfect.

105
00:08:12,770 --> 00:08:14,690
Had been? Past

106
00:08:14,690 --> 00:08:18,130
perfect. Will be? Future.

107
00:08:18,140 --> 00:08:18,620
Yeah.

108
00:08:19,430 --> 00:08:25,790
Again, we can also have forms like to be which is the infinitive of be.

109
00:08:26,030 --> 00:08:37,250
We can have being which is the gerund form, the -ing form of be and we can also use be with modals.

110
00:08:37,250 --> 00:08:45,470
So we add a modal verb to show some kind of detail, to introduce a detail into the sentence.

111
00:08:45,470 --> 00:08:51,680
So can be, may be, must be, should be whatever modal verb.

112
00:08:53,150 --> 00:08:56,870
So basically be does the job.

113
00:08:57,170 --> 00:09:05,630
What you have to remember that in passive voice, the verb be does all of the job, because it shows us

114
00:09:05,630 --> 00:09:13,280
which tense we we mean in the sentence, which tense we are using.

115
00:09:13,670 --> 00:09:16,300
And what about past participle then?

116
00:09:16,310 --> 00:09:23,060
Well, that's very easy here, because past participle is always the same.

117
00:09:23,060 --> 00:09:33,920
Whatever tense we use in this part with be, it does not have any effect, doesn't change anything about

118
00:09:33,920 --> 00:09:34,580
this part.

119
00:09:34,580 --> 00:09:36,350
So this part is fixed.

120
00:09:36,350 --> 00:09:38,420
It's always like that.

121
00:09:38,420 --> 00:09:46,550
If it's passive voice, the verb, the main verb of the sentence, the main action that we express is

122
00:09:46,550 --> 00:09:48,290
always in past participle.

123
00:09:48,290 --> 00:09:58,460
So it's, for example, done, fixed, read, reported, launched, used, awarded and so on and so

124
00:09:58,460 --> 00:09:58,760
on.

125
00:09:59,060 --> 00:10:07,760
So any verb, any action that you express by that sentence is going to be automatically past participle,

126
00:10:07,760 --> 00:10:10,460
So -ed or the third form.

127
00:10:12,350 --> 00:10:16,280
So this is the general overview of the formation.

128
00:10:16,280 --> 00:10:26,060
Now if we add some kind of a beginning to, to this, to this picture, the beginning of the sentence,

129
00:10:26,060 --> 00:10:35,750
which is the object on which the action is going to be directed, then we can try to play with it and

130
00:10:36,170 --> 00:10:39,710
make up some example sentences out of this picture.

131
00:10:39,710 --> 00:10:41,750
So let's try to do just that.

132
00:10:42,690 --> 00:10:46,290
For example, I'm going to take the first one.

133
00:10:46,440 --> 00:10:49,590
A task is done.

134
00:10:51,120 --> 00:10:52,050
So here you go.

135
00:10:52,050 --> 00:10:55,050
We have a sentence in the passive voice.

136
00:10:55,230 --> 00:10:56,820
A task is done.

137
00:10:56,820 --> 00:11:01,740
The action is done, is directed to the task.

138
00:11:01,980 --> 00:11:04,710
So it's performed upon the task.

139
00:11:05,970 --> 00:11:06,450
Good.

140
00:11:07,050 --> 00:11:13,980
Let's try to play with it some more and let's make up a couple of more sentences in different forms.

141
00:11:14,760 --> 00:11:19,090
So let's look at that and try to do it yourself.

142
00:11:19,110 --> 00:11:21,240
Try to make up two or three sentences.

143
00:11:22,790 --> 00:11:27,140
If you need some more time, you can pause the video, play with it, and then resume.

144
00:11:28,460 --> 00:11:30,500
So let's go on. The app.

145
00:11:30,530 --> 00:11:33,500
Let's use past simple tense.

146
00:11:33,530 --> 00:11:36,740
The app was fixed.

147
00:11:37,670 --> 00:11:39,740
Okay, the application was fixed.

148
00:11:39,740 --> 00:11:41,920
This is a passive sentence in past

149
00:11:41,960 --> 00:11:46,100
simple. The bug will be

150
00:11:47,050 --> 00:11:51,280
fixed. or The bug will be reported.

151
00:11:51,280 --> 00:11:52,810
Just to use another word.

152
00:11:53,890 --> 00:11:57,820
A laptop has been used.

153
00:11:59,470 --> 00:11:59,920
Okay.

154
00:11:59,920 --> 00:12:00,820
Sounds good.

155
00:12:00,970 --> 00:12:04,870
Our team can be awarded.

156
00:12:06,650 --> 00:12:07,270
All right.

157
00:12:07,280 --> 00:12:10,280
So we used all of the options from here.

158
00:12:10,640 --> 00:12:13,070
But I hope you get the general idea.

159
00:12:13,160 --> 00:12:21,470
The thing that does the most of the jobs here with passive voice is the verb be, and it can be in the

160
00:12:21,470 --> 00:12:23,090
variety of forms.

161
00:12:24,950 --> 00:12:25,280
Good.

162
00:12:25,760 --> 00:12:32,750
Now, to give some more structure to this knowledge, let's look at this table.

163
00:12:32,840 --> 00:12:41,570
So the table shows us tenses, starting from continuous, perfect, and then future simple tense.

164
00:12:42,320 --> 00:12:48,020
So let's start with present continuous. In the second column of

165
00:12:48,620 --> 00:12:52,000
this table you will have the active sentence

166
00:12:52,010 --> 00:12:58,580
so the sentence in active voice. And the third column shows us the sentence in the passive voice.

167
00:12:59,300 --> 00:13:01,310
And let's see what we have.

168
00:13:01,820 --> 00:13:06,920
Performance logging is used when you are fixing performance issues.

169
00:13:09,490 --> 00:13:18,780
What interests us here is this part you are fixing. It is present continuous tense.

170
00:13:18,790 --> 00:13:23,290
You are fixing. Now what happens in the passive?

171
00:13:24,070 --> 00:13:29,470
Performance logging is used when performance issues are being fixed.

172
00:13:30,950 --> 00:13:31,180
Okay.

173
00:13:31,340 --> 00:13:33,050
Now let's look at this

174
00:13:33,200 --> 00:13:42,680
these two parts, when the present continuous shows up, the active - you are fixing - and the passive - issues

175
00:13:42,680 --> 00:13:44,600
are being fixed.

176
00:13:46,440 --> 00:13:47,970
What can we see here?

177
00:13:48,210 --> 00:13:51,360
Well, let's start from the main verb.

178
00:13:52,080 --> 00:14:02,370
We know that the main verb of the sentence, when we want to make a passive sentence always is going

179
00:14:02,370 --> 00:14:06,840
to be in past participle form.

180
00:14:06,990 --> 00:14:09,030
Yeah, we know it's fixed.

181
00:14:09,030 --> 00:14:11,880
It's something that doesn't change. Here

182
00:14:11,880 --> 00:14:13,470
we can see it in that slide, yeah

183
00:14:14,130 --> 00:14:14,970
It's fixed.

184
00:14:14,970 --> 00:14:16,350
It's always like that.

185
00:14:16,350 --> 00:14:18,360
We don't have any options here.

186
00:14:18,360 --> 00:14:20,130
It's always past participle.

187
00:14:21,570 --> 00:14:23,550
And here we see it, of course.

188
00:14:23,700 --> 00:14:27,090
So fixing changes into fixed.

189
00:14:29,620 --> 00:14:39,760
But if we're just change fixing into fixed, how would we know that this sentence is in present continuous?

190
00:14:40,240 --> 00:14:48,370
It means that this -ing that is left out here needs to be added to something else, to another verb.

191
00:14:48,850 --> 00:14:52,690
And that other verb is, of course, our be.

192
00:14:53,020 --> 00:14:57,400
So you know that be can take a variety of different forms.

193
00:14:58,030 --> 00:15:01,180
So be takes the -ing ending.

194
00:15:02,240 --> 00:15:02,750
Okay.

195
00:15:02,750 --> 00:15:12,440
And now the are, we've had are here in active, and it stays here with us for the passive voice.

196
00:15:12,440 --> 00:15:16,400
So are just goes from here to here

197
00:15:16,400 --> 00:15:25,370
Right. Then we add this -ing to the verb be and "are being" is our present continuous form.

198
00:15:26,060 --> 00:15:32,660
You see are being, its present continues and the passive voice is shown by this fixed.

199
00:15:33,260 --> 00:15:36,410
So we have the form - are being fixed.

200
00:15:37,190 --> 00:15:44,930
The thing is that in active voice the formation is always a little bit more concise, so a little smaller

201
00:15:44,930 --> 00:15:54,710
we have just two elements - are fixing - in passive voice because we have be the formation now is composed

202
00:15:54,710 --> 00:15:57,650
of three words - are being fixed.

203
00:15:59,790 --> 00:16:00,160
Good.

204
00:16:00,520 --> 00:16:02,200
Let's go to past continuous.

205
00:16:02,200 --> 00:16:05,860
Past continuous is exactly the same situation.

206
00:16:06,550 --> 00:16:17,560
Just instead of are or is or am we have was or were. Our developer was collecting data to assess program

207
00:16:17,560 --> 00:16:18,490
performance.

208
00:16:19,810 --> 00:16:26,860
The passive voice would be The data were being collected to assess program performance.

209
00:16:29,420 --> 00:16:38,750
I hope that you can see again, this transformation that happened here. Was and were it's the same form

210
00:16:38,750 --> 00:16:40,550
for past continuous. 

211
00:16:40,580 --> 00:16:47,750
The difference is that we start with the doer, which is the developer, and he is one person.

212
00:16:47,750 --> 00:16:48,980
So it's singular.

213
00:16:49,010 --> 00:16:51,710
That's why we use was. The data

214
00:16:51,710 --> 00:16:59,000
we take it as the general concept and we use it in plural with a plural verb.

215
00:16:59,420 --> 00:17:06,170
That's why was changed into were but what comes to the tense is the same.

216
00:17:07,550 --> 00:17:16,160
And then -ing goes to be and we have being and collected in the past participle form.

217
00:17:18,420 --> 00:17:23,790
Now let's move down to to perfect, perfect tenses.

218
00:17:24,300 --> 00:17:27,119
Present perfect, active voice.

219
00:17:27,329 --> 00:17:31,110
We have already done all the tasks for this sprint.

220
00:17:32,400 --> 00:17:33,630
Passive voice.

221
00:17:33,660 --> 00:17:38,250
All the tasks for this sprint have already been done.

222
00:17:40,710 --> 00:17:42,450
Let's inspect here.

223
00:17:43,110 --> 00:17:47,220
So the active form is - have already done.

224
00:17:48,180 --> 00:17:52,320
The passive form is - have already been done.

225
00:17:53,670 --> 00:17:55,170
So what happens here?

226
00:17:55,830 --> 00:18:01,800
We have done as the main verb in the active sentence.

227
00:18:02,640 --> 00:18:12,090
However, because present perfect already uses past participle in its formation, we don't need to do

228
00:18:12,090 --> 00:18:12,960
anything to it.

229
00:18:12,960 --> 00:18:14,460
It's already past participle.

230
00:18:14,760 --> 00:18:16,530
So we just take it as it is.

231
00:18:16,530 --> 00:18:18,630
The main verb is in past participle.

232
00:18:18,750 --> 00:18:19,950
Here we have it.

233
00:18:20,970 --> 00:18:29,010
Now the word have as the auxiliary verb for present perfect stays the same.

234
00:18:29,650 --> 00:18:31,450
So we have it here.

235
00:18:31,450 --> 00:18:32,470
Yes, have.

236
00:18:32,710 --> 00:18:37,960
And then somehow we should show that this is passive.

237
00:18:37,960 --> 00:18:40,210
Yes, this is passive and present 

238
00:18:40,210 --> 00:18:40,870
perfect.

239
00:18:42,390 --> 00:18:49,620
Present perfect made is made up of have/has and past participle.

240
00:18:50,450 --> 00:18:51,830
Have and done.

241
00:18:52,130 --> 00:18:59,150
But this done here is not because of present perfect, but because of passive voice.

242
00:18:59,510 --> 00:19:07,460
So another verb needs to show that it's present perfect and that another verb, and you know it

243
00:19:07,460 --> 00:19:17,000
the verb that does all the job, always, in all of the sentences, is be, so be takes the the past participle

244
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:17,660
form.

245
00:19:18,020 --> 00:19:19,880
So it's in the third column.

246
00:19:20,660 --> 00:19:27,380
That's why we have again, three elements - have been done. In active voice

247
00:19:27,380 --> 00:19:30,530
we would have two elements - have done.

248
00:19:33,170 --> 00:19:33,830
Now past

249
00:19:33,860 --> 00:19:34,700
perfect.

250
00:19:35,410 --> 00:19:39,730
It's the same situation instead of have we have had.

251
00:19:40,270 --> 00:19:44,950
So The company had fired him long before they hired me.

252
00:19:45,980 --> 00:19:51,500
Passive sentence would be He had been fired long before they hired me.

253
00:19:53,220 --> 00:20:03,630
Okay, I think that is clear here - had fired changes into had been fired. Again fired and fired, because

254
00:20:03,630 --> 00:20:04,980
it's past participle.

255
00:20:04,980 --> 00:20:16,440
But this past participle shows the passive voice and this part - had been - is the copy of had fired just

256
00:20:16,440 --> 00:20:22,530
instead of fired we use been, so been takes the perfect form.

257
00:20:23,190 --> 00:20:26,340
So the third column, had been fired.

258
00:20:28,360 --> 00:20:28,810
Future

259
00:20:28,810 --> 00:20:29,530
simple.

260
00:20:30,370 --> 00:20:32,830
We will finish the project soon.

261
00:20:32,860 --> 00:20:37,030
It's the active sentence. And the passive sentence would sound

262
00:20:37,060 --> 00:20:39,520
The project will be finished soon.

263
00:20:40,450 --> 00:20:41,150
That's easy 

264
00:20:41,170 --> 00:20:51,760
here. Again, the main verb - finish - changes into finished, because it's past participle for passive and finish

265
00:20:51,760 --> 00:20:56,170
is the infinitive in this case.

266
00:20:56,830 --> 00:21:05,500
So be takes the form of infinitive instead of finish and will just shows us the future

267
00:21:05,500 --> 00:21:06,010
simple.

268
00:21:06,010 --> 00:21:07,990
So will stays with us.

269
00:21:08,080 --> 00:21:09,850
Will be finished.

270
00:21:12,530 --> 00:21:12,920
Okay

271
00:21:12,920 --> 00:21:15,120
Now it's quite a lot.

272
00:21:15,150 --> 00:21:19,280
Yeah, the formation is a little bit more complex.

273
00:21:19,700 --> 00:21:28,220
However, if you understand just this, this concept of how they are transformed, it's a lot easier

274
00:21:28,220 --> 00:21:30,470
than to use all of those forms.

275
00:21:30,470 --> 00:21:41,270
You just have to understand that whatever form the main verb has in active sentence, the same form

276
00:21:41,270 --> 00:21:45,200
is going to be for "be" in the passive sentence.

277
00:21:46,560 --> 00:21:46,780
Okay

278
00:21:46,860 --> 00:21:56,190
So be takes the form of the main verb, and the main verb in the passive is always the same.

279
00:21:56,340 --> 00:21:59,100
It's always past participle form.

280
00:21:59,370 --> 00:22:02,700
If you look finish changes into finished.

281
00:22:03,360 --> 00:22:04,320
Fired -

282
00:22:04,320 --> 00:22:05,190
fired.

283
00:22:05,670 --> 00:22:06,810
These are perfect.

284
00:22:06,810 --> 00:22:08,010
Done - done.

285
00:22:08,760 --> 00:22:12,720
Collecting - collected, fixing - fixed.

286
00:22:12,750 --> 00:22:25,380
So every time the main verb in passive is in past participle, but be verb takes care of all the tense

287
00:22:25,380 --> 00:22:33,630
forms - will be, had been, have been, were being, are being and so on.

288
00:22:33,630 --> 00:22:44,850
And if we go back to those simpler tenses, are, yes, and was, again the verb be just takes the form of whatever

289
00:22:44,850 --> 00:22:46,320
tense we are talking about.

290
00:22:48,480 --> 00:22:48,990
Good.

291
00:22:49,020 --> 00:22:50,670
Now, these were the tenses.

292
00:22:50,670 --> 00:23:01,080
And also we can use passive voice not only with tenses, but also with modals and infinitives or gerunds.

293
00:23:01,350 --> 00:23:08,850
So if we have any modal, the active sentence would be, for example We can't do it now.

294
00:23:08,880 --> 00:23:10,650
Doesn't have to be can.

295
00:23:10,660 --> 00:23:19,620
You can say may or might, must or should, whatever modal verb. So we can't do.

296
00:23:19,710 --> 00:23:28,530
And after the modal verb we use the infinitive, always the infinitive without anything, without to.

297
00:23:29,010 --> 00:23:35,090
So this infinitive will be the verb be for passive.

298
00:23:35,760 --> 00:23:39,090
It can be done now.

299
00:23:39,120 --> 00:23:40,590
It can't be done now.

300
00:23:41,190 --> 00:23:43,680
So it's as simple as that for modals.

301
00:23:44,220 --> 00:23:54,060
And when we have the -ing form of the verb, for example She doesn't like people criticizing her.

302
00:23:55,320 --> 00:23:56,580
Criticizing.

303
00:23:56,940 --> 00:23:58,440
Yeah, we have criticizing.

304
00:23:58,440 --> 00:24:04,260
And in the passive, we say She doesn't like being criticized.

305
00:24:05,160 --> 00:24:13,220
So again, the -ing form of the main verb goes to the be verb - being.

306
00:24:13,230 --> 00:24:16,950
And of course criticized is in past participle.

307
00:24:18,570 --> 00:24:21,630
The same situation is with infinitive with to.

308
00:24:22,350 --> 00:24:28,320
App creators want users to give feedback on new apps features.

309
00:24:29,400 --> 00:24:34,020
Apps creators want to be given feedback. Want

310
00:24:34,020 --> 00:24:37,020
to be given. Again,

311
00:24:37,200 --> 00:24:40,590
to give is the infinitive.

312
00:24:41,070 --> 00:24:49,890
So be becomes this infinitive - to be, and given goes to the third form give - gave - given.

313
00:24:50,070 --> 00:24:53,310
So we have the third column, because it's passive.

314
00:24:53,880 --> 00:24:58,110
App creators want to be given feedback on new apps features.

